<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">François-Charles de Velbruck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1719">1719</a>–<a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">François-Charles de Velbruck was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Liège from 1772 to 1784. An enlightened ruler, he promoted education, public health, poor relief and the arts, and supported the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/societe-libre-d-emulation">Société libre d'Émulation</a> in 1779. His tolerant rule contrasted with that of his successor Hoensbroeck.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francois-charles-de-velbruck.jpg" alt="François-Charles de Velbruck" width="284" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lambert-Joseph Gregoire · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francois-Charles_de_Velbruck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Velbrück was born at Garath Castle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> on 11 June 1719. In 1735, at the age of 16, he received from his brother a prebend of Saint Lambert's Cathedral in Liège, and a year later he was received onto the cathedral chapter by procuration, though he did not live in Liège until 1745. In 1756 he became archdeacon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a> and, later that year, keeper of the prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seal.</p><p>In 1757 he led a diplomatic mission to the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and was made a canon of Münster Cathedral. Two years later he became grand master of the household and prime minister to Johann Theodor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Liège. A conduit of French influence, he lost his place after Johann Theodor's death in 1763 and the election of his rival, Charles-Nicolas d'Oultremont. In 1765 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> made him commendatory abbot of Saint-Nicolas at Cheminon in Champagne. On Oultremont's death in 1772 he was elected unanimously to succeed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>As prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> he tried to bring the ideas of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> to the Principality of Liège, though a lack of money and power left many projects unfinished. He set up the Hôpital général Saint-Léonard for the needy and a free course in midwifery, and created free charity schools for poor children. In 1773 he carried out the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in Liège, and he gave more weight in teaching to the physical sciences, mathematics and the human sciences.</p><p>He planned a large public library and in 1774 began building a public academy of painting, sculpture and engraving. In 1779 he founded the Société littéraire de Liège and the Société d'Emulation, where the city's scholars met foreign visitors and heard of scientific discoveries and new works of art and poetry. It was later claimed that he was a freemason, but the historian of the Liège lodges found no proof of it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Velbrück died at Hex Castle, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a>, on 30 April 1784 and was buried in Liège. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liege-revolution">Liège Revolution</a> his remains, unlike those of his predecessors, were not thrown into a ditch, and his mausoleum survived. Restored, it has stood since 15 June 2000 in the cloister of St. Paul's Cathedral, Liège. Its epitaph praises him as both an Augustus and a Maecenas to the people of Liège.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “François-Charles de Velbrück” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Enlightened prince-bishops such as <b>François-Charles de Velbruck</b> (1772–1784) encouraged education and the Société d’Émulation, founded in 1779, but his successor Constantin-François de Hoensbroeck faced growing opposition, sharpened by a dispute over the licensing of gaming houses at Spa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Schloss Garath, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Liège, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.14,6.91&amp;year=1761" aria-label="Schloss Garath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4707 391 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of François-Charles de Velbruck"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5157 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5157 661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.14,6.91&amp;year=1761">What was here in 1761? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
